[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. So this cycle designs the [1mtask layer[0m, with the explicit job of producing a change-event stream that
  notifications can consume next.

  The most consequential question for that layer is where it runs, because "users get notified about tasks they care
  about" implies more than one user, while what's in the repo is a static page with no server.

  - [1mStatic, single-user, browser-only[0m — tasks live in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, no accounts, no network. Buys a working app today
    with zero infrastructure; costs real multi-user notifications entirely — you can only notify the one person sitting
    at the browser. Reversible only by rewriting the storage and identity layers.
  - [1mServer-backed, multi-user[0m — a small backend owns tasks and users, the page talks to it. Buys genuine "other people
    changed your task" notifications, which is what your request describes; costs choosing a stack, storage, and auth
    now. This is the expensive-to-reverse direction, in both directions.
  - [1mLocal-first now, server-ready boundary[0m — still browser-only storage, but tasks go through a repository interface and
    every mutation emits a change event, so swapping in a server later doesn't touch the UI. Buys deferral; costs some
    indirection that's dead weight if you never add a server.

  My recommendation is [1mserver-backed[0m — but only if multi-user is genuinely the goal. Notifications between users cannot
  be faked on a static page, and the local-first middle road tends to buy less deferral than it promises, because auth
  and conflict handling are the hard parts and it defers exactly those. If the real goal is a personal task list with
  reminders, that's a different and much smaller system, and static is the right answer.
